eceiveInteraction"><l><option>clicked</option></l></block><block s="doIf"><block s="reportVariadicAnd"><list><custom-block s="humans turn?"></custom-block><block s="reportVariadicEquals"><list><block s="getCostumeIdx"></block><l>1</l></list></block></list></block><script><custom-block s="move in this square"></custom-block></script><list></list></block></script><script x="16.666666666666657" y="833.7777777777773"><custom-block s="next move for computer"><comment w="112.66666666666667" collapsed="true">5.6.4.B (TIF) inside</comment></custom-block></script><comment x="12.333333333333343" y="566.6666666666667" w="364.6666666666667" collapsed="false">5.6.4.A (TIF):  Here&apos;s one way.  Human plays X.&#xD;&#xD;X: square 1 (not 5!)&#xD;O: square 5 (best empty square)&#xD;X: square 9 (counterintuitive, because triple 159 can&apos;t be won)&#xD;O: square 3 (best empty square)&#xD;X: square 7 (forced move, but that&apos;s okay...)&#xD;O: square 8 (forced move, but too late, because X had /two/ ways to win!)&#xD;X: square 4 (WIN)&#xD;&#xD;X&apos;s first two moves are counterintuitive, but the computer&apos;s strategy rules don&apos;t know how to cope with them.  After X&apos;s first move, O must move anywhere but square 5 or square 9 in order to achieve a tie game.&#xD;&#xD;The rule developed in TIF B doesn&apos;t protect against this opening.  For that, the computer must specifically recognize a corner opening and respond to it with a specific rule.  The TIF B rule is good for turning what might be ties into wins, if the human opponent makes any mistakes.</comment></scripts></sprite><watcher scope="Square" var="position number" style="normal" x="10" y="298.500002" color="243,118,29" hidden="true"/><watcher var="playing" style="normal" x="10" y="325.499996" color="243,118,29" hidden="true"/><watcher var="win lines" style="normal" x="10" y="177.999998" color="243,118,29" hidden="true"/><watcher var="board" style="normal" x="0" y="27" color="243,118,29" extX="80" extY="70"/><